WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:02.785
SANDEEP DINESH: The first step
in deploying to Kubernetes

00:00:02.785 --> 00:00:04.610
is putting your app
inside a container.

00:00:04.610 --> 00:00:06.290
But why stop there?

00:00:06.290 --> 00:00:08.850
In this episode of
"Kubernetes Best Practices,"

00:00:08.850 --> 00:00:11.990
let's explore how you can create
small and secure container

00:00:11.990 --> 00:00:12.927
images.

00:00:12.927 --> 00:00:16.265
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:21.050 --> 00:00:23.360
Thanks to Docker,
creating container images

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:24.980
has never been simpler.

00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:26.660
Specify your base image.

00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:29.850
Add your changes, and
build your container.

00:00:29.850 --> 00:00:32.600
While this is great for getting
started, using the default

00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:35.960
base images can lead to
large images full of security

00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:37.190
vulnerabilities.

00:00:37.190 --> 00:00:41.049
Most Docker images use Debian
or Ubuntu as the base image.

00:00:41.049 --> 00:00:42.590
While this is great
for compatibility

00:00:42.590 --> 00:00:44.990
and easy onboarding,
these base images

00:00:44.990 --> 00:00:47.540
can add hundreds of megabytes
of additional overhead

00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:48.980
to your container.

00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:51.600
For example, simple
Node.js and Go,

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:56.390
"hello world" apps are
around 700 megabytes.

00:00:56.390 --> 00:00:59.540
Your application is probably
only a few megabytes in size.

00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:01.250
So all this
additional overhead is

00:01:01.250 --> 00:01:03.980
wasted space and a
great hiding place

00:01:03.980 --> 00:01:06.890
for security
vulnerabilities and bugs.

00:01:06.890 --> 00:01:09.740
So let's look at two methods
to reduce the container image

00:01:09.740 --> 00:01:11.820
size--

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:16.860
using small base images and
using the builder patcher.

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:19.590
Using smaller base images
is probably the easiest way

00:01:19.590 --> 00:01:21.630
to reduce your container size.

00:01:21.630 --> 00:01:24.630
Chances are your language
or stack that you are using

00:01:24.630 --> 00:01:26.160
provides an official
image that's

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:29.100
much smaller than
the default image.

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:33.000
For example, let's take a
look at our Node.js container.

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:37.410
Going from the default node:8 to
node:8-alpine reduces our base

00:01:37.410 --> 00:01:39.930
image size by 10 times.

00:01:39.930 --> 00:01:42.590
To move to a smaller base
image, update your Docker

00:01:42.590 --> 00:01:45.130
file to start with
a new base image.

00:01:45.130 --> 00:01:47.640
Now, unlike the
old onbuild image,

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:49.830
you need to copy your
code into the container

00:01:49.830 --> 00:01:53.450
and install any dependencies.

00:01:53.450 --> 00:01:55.670
In the new Docker file,
the container starts with

00:01:55.670 --> 00:01:59.390
the node:alpine image, creates
a directory for the code,

00:01:59.390 --> 00:02:02.000
install dependencies
with NPM, and finally,

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:04.280
starts the Node.js server.

00:02:04.280 --> 00:02:06.590
With this update, the
resulting container

00:02:06.590 --> 00:02:09.050
is almost 10 times smaller.

00:02:09.050 --> 00:02:10.639
If your programming
language or stack

00:02:10.639 --> 00:02:13.040
doesn't have an option
for a small base image,

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:15.180
you can build your
container using raw Alpine

00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:17.190
Linux as a starting point.

00:02:17.190 --> 00:02:19.040
This also gives you
complete control

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:21.740
over what goes inside
your containers.

00:02:21.740 --> 00:02:23.660
Now, using a small base
image is a great way

00:02:23.660 --> 00:02:25.730
to quickly build
small containers.

00:02:25.730 --> 00:02:28.430
But you might be able to go
even smaller using the builder

00:02:28.430 --> 00:02:30.590
pattern.

00:02:30.590 --> 00:02:32.930
With interpretive
languages, the source code

00:02:32.930 --> 00:02:36.410
is sent to an interpreter, and
then it is executed directly.

00:02:36.410 --> 00:02:39.140
But with a compiled
language, the source code

00:02:39.140 --> 00:02:41.960
is turned into compiled
code beforehand.

00:02:41.960 --> 00:02:44.690
Now, with compile languages,
the compilation step

00:02:44.690 --> 00:02:47.000
often requires tools
that are not needed

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:48.935
to actually run the code.

00:02:48.935 --> 00:02:50.810
So this means that you
can remove these tools

00:02:50.810 --> 00:02:53.120
from the final
container completely.

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:56.640
To do this, you can use
the builder pattern.

00:02:56.640 --> 00:02:59.250
The code is built in
the first container,

00:02:59.250 --> 00:03:03.120
and then the compiled code is
packaged in the final container

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:05.670
without all the compilers
and tools required

00:03:05.670 --> 00:03:08.410
to make the compiled code.

00:03:08.410 --> 00:03:12.190
So let's take a Go application
through this process.

00:03:12.190 --> 00:03:16.890
First, let's move from the
onbuild image to Alpine Linux.

00:03:16.890 --> 00:03:19.310
In the new Docker file,
the container starts with

00:03:19.310 --> 00:03:21.870
a golang:alpine image.

00:03:21.870 --> 00:03:25.410
Then it creates a directory for
the code, copies in the source

00:03:25.410 --> 00:03:30.570
code, builds the source, and
then finally starts the app.

00:03:30.570 --> 00:03:33.510
This container is much smaller
than the onbuild container,

00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:36.150
but it still contains a
compiler and other Go tools

00:03:36.150 --> 00:03:37.860
that we really don't need.

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:40.830
Let's extract just the
compiled program out and put it

00:03:40.830 --> 00:03:43.770
into its own container.

00:03:43.770 --> 00:03:46.940
So you might notice something
strange about this Docker file.

00:03:46.940 --> 00:03:49.640
It has two FROM lines.

00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:53.540
The first section looks exactly
the same as the previous Docker

00:03:53.540 --> 00:03:56.450
file, except that it
uses the AS keyword

00:03:56.450 --> 00:03:58.700
to give this step a name.

00:03:58.700 --> 00:04:01.760
In the next section,
there is a new FROM line.

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:05.530
This will start a fresh
image, and instead of using

00:04:05.530 --> 00:04:09.200
golang:alpine, we will use
raw alpine as the base image.

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:13.230
Raw Alpine Linux doesn't have
any SSL certificates installed,

00:04:13.230 --> 00:04:16.850
which will make most API
calls over HTTPS fail.

00:04:16.850 --> 00:04:19.279
So let's install some
root CA certificates.

00:04:19.279 --> 00:04:21.110
And now comes the
interesting part.

00:04:21.110 --> 00:04:24.140
You can use the COPY command
to copy the compiled code

00:04:24.140 --> 00:04:27.080
from the first container
into the second.

00:04:27.080 --> 00:04:29.900
This line will copy just
that one file and not

00:04:29.900 --> 00:04:31.730
the rest of the Go tooling.

00:04:31.730 --> 00:04:34.850
This new multistage Docker
file contains a container image

00:04:34.850 --> 00:04:37.070
that's just 12 megabytes.

00:04:37.070 --> 00:04:40.820
The original container
image was 700 megabytes.

00:04:40.820 --> 00:04:42.440
That is quite a difference.

00:04:42.440 --> 00:04:45.050
Using small base images
and the builder pattern

00:04:45.050 --> 00:04:47.780
are great ways to create
much smaller containers

00:04:47.780 --> 00:04:49.135
without a lot of work.

00:04:49.135 --> 00:04:51.262
Now, depending on your
application stack,

00:04:51.262 --> 00:04:53.720
there may be additional ways
to reduce your container image

00:04:53.720 --> 00:04:56.300
size as well.

00:04:56.300 --> 00:05:00.670
But do small containers actually
have a measurable advantage?

00:05:00.670 --> 00:05:03.750
Let's look at two areas where
small containers shine--

00:05:03.750 --> 00:05:06.620
performance and security.

00:05:06.620 --> 00:05:08.510
For performance,
let's look at how long

00:05:08.510 --> 00:05:11.390
it takes to build a container,
push it to a registry,

00:05:11.390 --> 00:05:14.180
and then pull it down
from the registry.

00:05:14.180 --> 00:05:15.890
For the initial
build, you can see

00:05:15.890 --> 00:05:19.010
that the smaller container has
a huge advantage over larger

00:05:19.010 --> 00:05:20.150
containers.

00:05:20.150 --> 00:05:23.210
Docker will cache layers so
subsequent builds will take

00:05:23.210 --> 00:05:25.430
very little time for either.

00:05:25.430 --> 00:05:28.310
But for many CI systems that
folks use to build and test

00:05:28.310 --> 00:05:30.530
containers, they
don't cache layers,

00:05:30.530 --> 00:05:32.392
so there is a significant
time saving here.

00:05:32.392 --> 00:05:34.850
Just think about how many times
you're building and testing

00:05:34.850 --> 00:05:36.750
your code.

00:05:36.750 --> 00:05:38.270
Now that the container
is built, you

00:05:38.270 --> 00:05:39.894
need to push it to
a container registry

00:05:39.894 --> 00:05:42.140
so you can use it in
your Kubernetes cluster.

00:05:42.140 --> 00:05:44.390
I recommend using the
Google Container Registry.

00:05:44.390 --> 00:05:46.650
You only pay for the
raw storage and network.

00:05:46.650 --> 00:05:48.650
There is no additional
fee to manage containers.

00:05:48.650 --> 00:05:51.840
It's private and secure,
and it's lightning fast.

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:55.256
In fact, GCR uses many
tricks to speed up pushing.

00:05:55.256 --> 00:05:56.630
You can see that
the time to push

00:05:56.630 --> 00:05:58.490
both the containers
for the large machine

00:05:58.490 --> 00:06:00.230
is almost the same.

00:06:00.230 --> 00:06:03.020
This is because GCR uses a
global cache for common base

00:06:03.020 --> 00:06:06.680
images, meaning you don't
need to upload them at all.

00:06:06.680 --> 00:06:09.591
With the small machine, the
CPU becomes the bottleneck.

00:06:09.591 --> 00:06:11.840
As you can see, there is
still a significant advantage

00:06:11.840 --> 00:06:14.010
to use small containers.

00:06:14.010 --> 00:06:15.830
If you're using Google
Container Registry,

00:06:15.830 --> 00:06:18.680
I highly recommend using
Google Container Builder

00:06:18.680 --> 00:06:20.690
as part of your build system.

00:06:20.690 --> 00:06:23.990
As you can see, it's much
faster to build and push

00:06:23.990 --> 00:06:25.910
than even the large machine.

00:06:25.910 --> 00:06:28.536
And you get a 120 build
minutes free per day,

00:06:28.536 --> 00:06:30.410
which should be enough
to cover most people's

00:06:30.410 --> 00:06:32.060
container-building needs.

00:06:32.060 --> 00:06:35.000
Now comes the most important
performance metric--

00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:36.690
pulling the container.

00:06:36.690 --> 00:06:38.690
While you might not care
about the time it takes

00:06:38.690 --> 00:06:40.400
to build and push
a container, you

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:41.929
should really care
about the time

00:06:41.929 --> 00:06:43.220
it takes to pull the container.

00:06:43.220 --> 00:06:47.030
For example, let's say you have
a three node cluster and one

00:06:47.030 --> 00:06:48.980
of the nodes crashes.

00:06:48.980 --> 00:06:51.740
If you're using a managed system
like Google Kubernetes Engine,

00:06:51.740 --> 00:06:53.510
the system will
automatically spin up

00:06:53.510 --> 00:06:55.820
a new node to take its place.

00:06:55.820 --> 00:06:58.762
However, this new node
will be completely fresh

00:06:58.762 --> 00:07:00.470
and will have to pull
all your containers

00:07:00.470 --> 00:07:02.570
before it can start working.

00:07:02.570 --> 00:07:04.952
If it takes too long
to pull the containers,

00:07:04.952 --> 00:07:07.160
this is just time where your
cluster isn't performing

00:07:07.160 --> 00:07:09.540
as well as it should.

00:07:09.540 --> 00:07:11.690
Now, there are many cases
where this may occur,

00:07:11.690 --> 00:07:13.730
such as adding a new
node to your cluster,

00:07:13.730 --> 00:07:15.770
upgrading your nodes,
or even switching

00:07:15.770 --> 00:07:18.340
to a new container
for your deployments.

00:07:18.340 --> 00:07:21.350
So minimizing pull
times becomes key.

00:07:21.350 --> 00:07:23.330
You can easily tell
the smaller container

00:07:23.330 --> 00:07:25.872
is much faster than
the large container.

00:07:25.872 --> 00:07:27.830
And you're probably
running multiple containers

00:07:27.830 --> 00:07:31.490
on your Kubernetes cluster, so
these times can add up quickly.

00:07:31.490 --> 00:07:34.430
Using small, common base
images for your containers

00:07:34.430 --> 00:07:36.290
significantly speeds
up the deployment

00:07:36.290 --> 00:07:38.870
times and speed at
which new Kubernetes

00:07:38.870 --> 00:07:41.600
nodes can come online.

00:07:41.600 --> 00:07:43.610
Now let's look at security.

00:07:43.610 --> 00:07:45.890
People often say
that containers are

00:07:45.890 --> 00:07:48.500
more secure if they're smaller,
because they have less surface

00:07:48.500 --> 00:07:50.190
area for attacks.

00:07:50.190 --> 00:07:53.000
Let's see if this
is actually true.

00:07:53.000 --> 00:07:55.550
An awesome feature of
Google Container Registry

00:07:55.550 --> 00:07:57.740
is that it can automatically
scan your containers

00:07:57.740 --> 00:07:59.450
for vulnerabilities.

00:07:59.450 --> 00:08:02.240
So I built both the onbuild
and multistage containers

00:08:02.240 --> 00:08:03.694
a few months ago.

00:08:03.694 --> 00:08:05.360
Let's see if there's
any vulnerabilities

00:08:05.360 --> 00:08:08.180
in these old images.

00:08:08.180 --> 00:08:10.700
Wow, there's only 3
medium vulnerabilities

00:08:10.700 --> 00:08:15.220
for the small container,
but 16 critical and over 370

00:08:15.220 --> 00:08:17.060
for the larger container.

00:08:17.060 --> 00:08:19.004
If we drill into the
larger container,

00:08:19.004 --> 00:08:20.420
we can see that
most of the issues

00:08:20.420 --> 00:08:22.280
have nothing to
do with their app,

00:08:22.280 --> 00:08:25.400
but rather programs that
we're not even using.

00:08:25.400 --> 00:08:27.320
When people talk about
an increased surface

00:08:27.320 --> 00:08:30.960
area for attacks, this is
what they're referring to.

00:08:30.960 --> 00:08:33.090
So remember-- build
small containers.

00:08:33.090 --> 00:08:35.770
The performance and
security benefits are real.

00:08:35.770 --> 00:08:38.789
I'll see you on the next episode
of "Kubernetes Best Practices."

00:08:38.789 --> 00:08:41.839
[MUSIC PLAYING]

